, F4. Let's see if I can form any of the high-point patterns. For example, All Green is not applicable here. Maybe a Full Flush? But I have both Characters and Bams. Not a Full Flush. How about Pure Straight? If I can form three Chows in the same suit. For example, in Characters, if I can form W2-W4, W5-W7, W8-W9. Wait, no, Chows are three consecutive. So maybe W5-W6-W7, W6-W7-W8, W7-W8-W9. But I have W5, W6, W7, W8, W9. That's a sequence from 5 to 9. If I can form multiple Chows here. For example, W5-W6-W7, W6-W7-W8, W7-W8-W9. But each Chow requires three tiles. So if I have those, but I have one of each. That would require overlapping Chows, which isn't possible with single tiles. So maybe not. Alternatively, maybe split into W5-W6-W7 and W8-W9 as a pair? No, the pair is needed. Hmm.\n\nAnother possibility: All Chows. If I can form four Chows and a pair. Let's see. My current meld is B6-B7-B8. If I can form W5-W6-W7, then maybe W8-W9 as part of another Chow? But I only have one W8 and W9. Maybe not. Alternatively, if I can form W2-W3-W4 (but I don't have W3), W5-W6-W7, and W8-W9 as a pair. Not enough. Maybe I need to discard a tile to make room for a better meld.\n\nLooking at the valid actions: the tiles I can play are W9, W4, W6, B5, W7, F2, W8, F4, W5, W2, F1. Let's evaluate each.\n\nIf I play W9: That's a terminal tile. Maybe someone is waiting for it. But it's in my hand once. The unshown tiles have W9:3 left. Playing it might not be too risky. But if I can use W9 in a meld, that would be better. For example, if I have W7-W8-W9 as a Chow. But I have W7, W8, W9. If I can form that, but I need three consecutive. If I can Chi for that, but since I'm the one playing, maybe not. Alternatively, maybe keep W9 for a possible wait.\n\nPlaying W4: If I discard W4, maybe someone can use it for a Chow. But I have W2 and W4. If I keep W2 and W4, maybe I can form a Chow with W3 if it's discarded. But I don't have W3. So maybe playing W4 is safe.\n\nPlaying B5: This is a mi